      "citeproc": "report",
      "bibtex": "techreport",
      "ris": "RPRT",
      "resourceType": "Report"
     },
     "relatedIdentifiers": [],
     "relatedItems": [],
     "sizes": [
      "1"
     ],
     "formats": [
      "PDF"
     ],
     "version": null,
     "rightsList": [
      {
       "rightsUri": "https://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/doc/open-government-licence/version/3/",
       "rights": "Open Government Licence"
      }
     ],
     "descriptions": [
      {
       "descriptionType": "Abstract",
       "description": "Open area excavation carried out in two land parcels A moderate density of archaeology was uncovered during the excavation, dated primarily to the Romano British period with elements of post medieval detritus in the form of brick and roof tile fragments. Most notable were two corn dryers and a small stone floored structure. An isolated crouched inhumation possibly dating to the prehistoric period was recovered in Land Parcel C240108. The majority of the archaeological features pertain to field systems in both areas of investigation. In Land Parcel C24018, three circular paddocks, two with controlled entrances with the other more open. One sub-circular enclosure has no evidence of openings which suggests modifications or a different form of entrance. These potentially represent stock control rather than agricultural activities and likely relate to the linear features recorded in the western area. Additional curvilinear features were recorded in the northern part of the excavation area. A similar field system was recorded in Land Parcel C24019. The linear features in the central part suggest one phase whereas the linear features to the west of the area appear to have been re-cut to re-establish the boundaries or to aid with drainage. A corn dryer and small square structure with a stone flagged floor was recorded at the eastern end of the area."
      }
     ],
